text:
devops-alex: quick question — trying to make our chart prefer a Secret created by ExternalSecrets, then fall back to an explicit override in values, then to a hardcoded default. currently if the secret exists but the key is missing we end up with an empty env var. any pattern to get strict fallthrough?

sre-maria: usual approach is to use lookup to see if the Secret exists and has the key, then choose the source. helm templates can check for nil/empty but secrets created later (by external controller) complicate dry-rendering locally. example pattern: 

```tpl
{{- $ns := .Release.Namespace -}}
{{- $secret := lookup "v1" "Secret" $ns (default "" .Values.db.secretName) -}}
{{- $val := "" -}}
{{- if and $secret (index $secret.data "DB_URL") -}}
  {{- $val = (printf "%s" $secret.data.DB_URL | b64decode) -}}
{{- else if .Values.db.overrideUrl -}}
  {{- $val = .Values.db.overrideUrl -}}
{{- else -}}
  {{- $val = "postgres://fallback:pass@localhost:5432/app" -}}
{{- end -}}

env:
- name: DB_URL
  value: {{ quote $val }}
```

eng-platform-joe: two notes: (1) lookup returns API objects only at render time against the cluster you're templating for — so CI renders for PRs may not see ExternalSecret-created resources unless you point to the cluster. (2) If you want to keep valueFrom/secretKeyRef semantics (so k8s pulls secret at runtime), you can create a short template that emits valueFrom referencing the secret only when lookup finds the secret, otherwise emit a plain value field with the override/default. That avoids empty valueFrom blocks.

caroline: example for emitting either valueFrom or value: 

```tpl
{{- $s := lookup "v1" "Secret" .Release.Namespace .Values.db.secretName -}}
{{- if and $s (index $s.data "DB_URL") -}}
- name: DB_URL
  valueFrom:
    secretKeyRef:
      name: {{ .Values.db.secretName }}
      key: DB_URL
{{- else if .Values.db.overrideUrl -}}
- name: DB_URL
  value: {{ quote .Values.db.overrideUrl }}
{{- else -}}
- name: DB_URL
  value: "postgres://fallback:pass@localhost:5432/app"
{{- end -}}
```

infra-bot: lint:chartcheck found: templates using lookup will cause helm template --validate to depend on cluster; add a comment in chart README and a chart-test that uses a test cluster. See https://git.internal/tools/chartcheck/rules#lookup-cluster-dependency :warning:

devops-alex: ahhh good point re: CI. Our PR renders run helm template in CI sandbox that doesn't have ExternalSecrets. Might add a small mock secret in the charttest job.

sre-maria: one more gotcha — if the ExternalSecret controller creates the Secret after rollout, the pod restart timing matters. If you ship a value override and later the secret appears, pods may still have the env value baked in. Using valueFrom (when present) keeps k8s in charge. If you prefer runtime swap, prefer producing valueFrom when secret exists and avoid static values.

eng-platform-joe: TL;DR: use lookup to detect presence/key, render either valueFrom or value accordingly, document the cluster-dependency in README, and add a charttest that creates a fake secret for CI.

devops-alex: sweet — I'll sketch a PR with the lookup approach and add a small charttest job. ping me on review please. :pray:

caroline: lgtm, also consider adding an annotation on the deployment so we can force rollout when the secret changes (if you want update propagation).

devops-alex: noted. thanks all!
